t's five-point Republican lean and Crank's incumbency outweigh a favorable Democratic climate; raters unanimously place the race at Lean R and markets price Crank around 70%, while the lone recent poll, taken by a Democratic pollster, showed Crank ahead by a point.", "watch": "A fresh independent poll or a decisive shift in the national political environment could move the race back toward tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 66 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Jeff Crank (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Colorado's 5th District, centered on Colorado Springs, leans Republican by about five points, and Representative Jeff Crank is seeking re-election against Democrat Jessica Killin. All nine expert ratings favor Crank, most calling the race Lean Republican, and prediction markets give him roughly a two-in-three chance. The only recent poll, conducted for a Democratic sponsor in July, showed Crank ahead by a single point.\n\nKillin has out-raised the incumbent and holds slightly more cash on hand, and a favorable national climate for Democrats keeps the race competitive; a Trump-linked super PAC has already spent to defend Crank, and dueling attack ads have marked the campaign. A Libertarian and a Constitution candidate also appear on the ballot. The evidence points to a narrow but clear Republican edge, with enough uncertainty that a strong Democratic wave could tighten it further.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.332, "margin": -2.57}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.3239, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.3532, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.346, "margin": -3.0}]}